! CLAUSE 1's DS SURVIVES A PROTECTED BRANCH TARGET: the one
! consequence of the "suppressed without a test" correction that
! df_bb_readonly.strap names in its header and cannot witness.
!
! THE CASE.  A MODIFYING BRANCH ON BIT (bits 60-61 not both zero)
! whose addressed bit lies in a protected word, whose BRANCH TARGET
! also lies in the protected area, and with IF's MASK BIT ONE.  The
! bit says branch.  Printed p.52 (PDF 55 -- PDF page = printed page +
! 3) makes this one instruction answer to two different sentences at
! once, and the model must not let one eat the other.
!
! THE PRIMARY SOURCE, read first-hand off the page image at 200 dpi.
! Printed p.52, left column, under "Execution Suppressed" -- and note
! for the next reader that the sentence does NOT wrap onto printed
! p.53, as df_bb_readonly.strap's header says: it wraps to the TOP OF
! THE RIGHT-HAND COLUMN OF p.52 itself, where clause 3 ends "...zero.
! Indicator DF is actuated but does not cause suppression of the
! instruction."
!
!   "Another example is that, since a protected effective bit address
!   in BRANCH ON BIT could result in the destruction of information,
!   alert DS or DF may occur if:
!     1. The addressed bit is not specified to remain unconditionally
!        unchanged; that is, bits 60 and 61 of the instruction are not
!        both zero.  The instruction is suppressed without a test of
!        the addressed bit, and indicator DS is actuated."
!
! AND THE SENTENCE THAT MAKES "SUPPRESSED" AND "DS IS ACTUATED"
! COMPATIBLE, same page, same column, higher up -- the list of what
! "the suppression of an instruction must necessarily exclude":
!
!   "1. The actuation of those indicators, associated with the
!    instruction, of Class S, and indicators DF and IF when their mask
!    bits are zero."
!
! DS IS CLASS S.  The paragraph above that list says so by name:
! execution is suppressed on an alert which "2. Indicates that
! completion of the execution might destroy information (IJ, EXE, DS,
! DF, IF, or XF)", and then "The indicators actuated in these cases
! are of Class S (Figure 11)."  So suppression is not allowed to take
! the DS actuation with it, whatever else it takes.  That is the
! assertion this program makes, and it is the whole program.
!
! WHY THE THREE CONDITIONS TOGETHER.  In the model's BitBranch arm the
! bit change -- and clause 1's `setBit(ind, ind_DS, 1)` inside it --
! is gated by `ifSuppress`, p.32's class-2 suppression of a branch
! into a protected area: "If the branch address of a successful branch
! operation is such that control would pass into a protected area, the
! entire branch operation, including any counting, bit changing, or
! index alteration, is suppressed.  The address boundaries are ignored
! if the mask bit is zero" (printed p.32, left column, read
! first-hand).  Computed from `wouldBranch` -- the tested bit -- that
! gate fires here and the DS actuation vanishes with the bit change.
! Computed from `takeBranch` -- `wouldBranch && !noTest`, the machine
! having been ALLOWED to look at the bit -- it does not, because p.52
! already suppressed this instruction "without a test of the addressed
! bit" and there is no successful branch for p.32's class 2 to
! monitor.  p.32 says the same thing in the general case: "If an
! address which is associated with an operation is not actually used,
! it will not be monitored by the contents of the boundary registers.
! For example, the store instruction counter address prefixed to a
! branch operation is not monitored unless the branch is successful."
!
! WHY IT NEEDED ITS OWN PROGRAM.  df_bb_readonly.strap's block E is
! this case with IF's mask bit at ZERO, deliberately: a mask of one
! re-routes its `takeBranch -> wouldBranch` injection away from the
! `BB, $IF, $` row that witnesses its own finding.  Adding a block
! there would also move its PASS and invalidate the eight halt
! addresses recorded in its header.  ds_terminates_branch.strap runs
! the same instruction with an UNPROTECTED target; if_branch_alarm's
! block 6 runs a modifying BB into a protected target with the mask at
! one, but its DATA word is word 7, which p.31 says never signals --
! so in that block `takeBranch` and `wouldBranch` are equal and
! nothing discriminates.  No program in the suite sets all three.
!
! THE OBSERVABLE IS THE DESTINATION'S VALUE, NOT AN INDICATOR READ --
! df_fetch_suppress.strap's design choice, borrowed.  DS is
! "permanently masked on" (p.32), so its actuation MUST produce an
! interruption; entry 19 of the table is routed to a routine that
! marks word 6, and the program checks the MARKER.  A model that
! "actuated" DS without interrupting, or that set the neighbouring
! indicator, fails a marker check where it could pass an indicator
! check.
!
! THE MARKERS ARE WORDS 6, 7 AND 8 -- DS, DF and IF.  p.31: "References
! to locations 0 and 4 through 31, inclusive, never cause an address
! monitoring signal", so no marker store can itself signal.  IF has an
! entry because with its mask bit ONE the `takeBranch -> wouldBranch`
! injection at the ifSignal call really does interrupt, and without an
! entry it would run off into unloaded storage instead of halting at a
! check.
!
! THE SET-UP PLANTS ITS BIT WHILE DISABLED, p.32: "When the
! interruption system is disabled, address monitoring is
! ineffective... the DS, DF and IF indicators cannot be turned on when
! the system is disabled."  That is what lets the program put a one
! into a word it is about to protect.
!
! THE NOPS ARE NOT PADDING.  Each block reports by INTERRUPTING, and
! this model takes an interruption at a quiescent boundary rather than
! at p.48's instruction boundary; the NOPs are the slack.
!
! THE THREE BLOCKS ARE AN ATTRIBUTION TRIANGLE.  A alone would leave
! open which of the two extra conditions did the work, so B repeats it
! with the mask at zero and C repeats it with the target outside the
! area, and both must still actuate DS.  Only A's configuration can
! lose it.
!
! WEAKER STANDARD: hand-written from the manual, like the DF and DS
! programs beside it.  jb_ax5b routine 1260 TEST 8C is the deck oracle
! for DF and IF under the enable and mask bits, but none of its four
! cases uses BRANCH ON BIT as the offending reference, so p.52's
! clauses have no deck test in this corpus.
!
! SIX INJECTIONS, EACH OBSERVED (not predicted).  Each was applied to
! a pristine StretchExecute.bsv by a helper that ABORTS unless the old
! text occurs exactly once and PRINTS THE DIFF before building,
! because a substitution that silently fails to match prints ALL TESTS
! PASSED, which reads exactly like an honest "this does not
! discriminate".  Each anchor is a WHOLE LINE: the bare token
! `takeBranch` occurs four times in the arm and a token substitution
! would build a different mutant than the row claims.  The clean run's
! PASS is at half-word 4186 (004055.00).  Reproduce any row with
! `python3 line_addresses.py programs/df_bb_if_ds.strap`.
!
!   ifSuppress computed from            -> 4126  004017.00
!     `wouldBranch` instead of               BZB, 6.0, $ (A)
!     `takeBranch` (THE HEADLINE)            -- the bit-change block was
!                                               skipped, so clause 1's
!                                               DS actuation went with
!                                               it and DS's routine
!                                               never ran
!   `takeBranch` -> `wouldBranch` at    -> 4130  004021.00
!     the ifSignal call                      BB, 8.0, $ (A)
!                                            -- the instruction signalled
!                                               IF from a branch it was
!                                               never allowed to decide,
!                                               and with the mask ONE
!                                               that INTERRUPTS.  Not
!                                               reachable in
!                                               df_bb_readonly, whose
!                                               mask is zero: there the
!                                               same mutation only sets
!                                               a quiet indicator.
!   `takeBranch` -> `wouldBranch` at    -> 4130  004021.00
!     the `next = tgt` line                  BB, 8.0, $ (A)
!                                            -- SAME HALT AS THE ROW
!                                               ABOVE, and the reason is
!                                               worth knowing: the branch
!                                               IS taken, so execute()'s
!                                               shared commit point sees
!                                               `next != fallthrough`,
!                                               signals IF, finds the
!                                               mask at one and undoes
!                                               the branch itself.  So
!                                               the mutant never reaches
!                                               TGTIN -- df_bb_readonly,
!                                               at mask zero, is what
!                                               observes the arrival.
!   `storeViolates` dropped from        -> 4126  004017.00
!     noTest                                 BZB, 6.0, $ (A)
!                                            -- takeBranch becomes true,
!                                               so ifSuppress fires for
!                                               real and eats the DS
!   ind_DS -> ind_DF at clause 1's      -> 4126  004017.00
!     setBit (the 19/20 swap)                BZB, 6.0, $ (A)
!                                            -- DF's mask is zero here,
!                                               so nothing interrupted
!                                               and word 6 stayed clear
!   the bit change no longer            -> 4183  004053.40
!     suppressed: `if (storeViolates)        BZB, PROT.0, $
!     ... else writeWord` turned into        -- DS still actuated, so
!     `if (storeViolates) ...;` then            every marker check
!     an unconditional writeWord                passed; the DESTINATION'S
!                                               VALUE is what caught it
!
! THAT LAST ROW IS WHY THE VALUE CHECK IS THERE -- df_fetch_suppress's
! design choice.  A model that "suppressed" by writing anyway passes
! every indicator and marker check in this program and is caught only
! by reading the word back.
!
! WHAT IS NOT DISCRIMINATING, SAID RATHER THAN LEFT TO BE FOUND.
! Dropping `!ifSuppress` from the bit-change gate cannot be caught by
! this program: in the clean model ifSuppress is FALSE in every block
! here, so the mutation changes nothing.  if_branch_alarm.strap's
! block 6 is what holds that gate.
!
! AND WHICH CHECKS NO ROW REACHES.  Blocks B and C are controls and no
! row halts in them: every mutation that breaks the DS actuation breaks
! it in A first, and A comes first.  Their evidence is in the CLEAN
! run -- they show DS is actuated in both neighbouring configurations,
! so A's pass cannot be an accident of the mask bit or of the target's
! protection alone.  The `BB, 7.0, $` and `BB, $DF, $` rows in A are
! belt-and-braces for the same reason: the 19/20 swap is caught one row
! earlier, at the marker, because DF's mask is zero and a DF actuation
! cannot interrupt.
!
! Instructions: BD, BE, LVI, SV, BB, BB1, BBZ, BZB, B, NOP, BEW -- all
! implemented.

        SLC, (8)4000.0
START   BD,  $+.32                   ! disable: monitoring is ineffective
        BB1, PROT.0, $+1.0           ! plant bit 0 of PROT while the store
                                     !   can still land
        BZB, PROT.0, $               ! hang if it did not land
        LVI, $X13, TBL
        SV,  $X13, $IA
        LVI, $X13, PROT
        SV,  $X13, $LB               ! lower boundary := PROT
        LVI, $X13, ABOVE
        SV,  $X13, $UB               ! upper boundary := ABOVE
        BBZ, 3.57, $+1.0             ! control bit := 0: signal INSIDE
        BB1, 12.21, $+1.0            ! IF's mask bit := ONE
        BE,  $+.32                   ! enable: monitoring is now effective
!
! ---- A. protected word, protected target, IF's mask ONE ----------
!   p.52 clause 1: "The instruction is suppressed without a test of
!   the addressed bit, and indicator DS is actuated."  PROT.0 is ONE,
!   so an unsuppressed BBZ would branch to TGTIN and clear the bit;
!   TGTIN is inside the area and IF's mask is one, so p.32's class-2
!   suppression is armed as well.  It must not be reached, because
!   there was no successful branch to monitor -- and it must not take
!   the DS actuation with it, because p.52's exclusion list keeps
!   Class S indicators out of a suppression.
        BBZ, $IF,  $+1.0             ! start from IF off
        BBZ, $DF,  $+1.0             ! start from DF off
        BBZ, PROT.0, TGTIN           ! the subject instruction
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        BZB, 6.0,  $                 ! hang if DS's routine did not run:
                                     !   clause 1's actuation, lost if
                                     !   ifSuppress reads the tested bit
        BBZ, 6.0,  $+1.0
        BB,  8.0,  $                 ! hang if IF's routine ran: p.32
                                     !   monitors the branch address of
                                     !   SUCCESSFUL branches only
        BB,  $IF,  $                 ! hang if IF's indicator is on at all
        BB,  $DF,  $                 ! hang if a MODIFYING BB lit DF:
                                     !   clause 1 names DS
        BB,  7.0,  $                 ! hang if DF's routine ran
!
! ---- B. the same, with IF's mask ZERO ----------------------------
!   The control that attributes A's behaviour to the MASK rather than
!   to the protected target.  p.32: "The address boundaries are
!   ignored if the mask bit is zero", so class 2 cannot suppress
!   anything here and DS must be actuated for the plainest reason.
!   This is df_bb_readonly.strap's block E, repeated so this program
!   stands on its own.
        BBZ, 12.21, $+1.0            ! IF's mask bit := ZERO
        BBZ, $IF,  $+1.0
        BBZ, PROT.0, TGTIN
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        BZB, 6.0,  $                 ! hang if DS's routine did not run
        BBZ, 6.0,  $+1.0
        BB,  8.0,  $                 ! hang if IF's routine ran
        BB,  $IF,  $                 ! hang if IF's indicator is on
!
! ---- C. mask ONE again, but the TARGET is outside the area -------
!   The control that attributes A's behaviour to the protected TARGET
!   rather than to the mask.  Same instruction, same protected data
!   word, same mask bit; OUTTGT is above the upper boundary, so
!   ifViolates is false and the DS actuation can only be lost by a
!   model that gates it on something else entirely.
        BB1, 12.21, $+1.0            ! IF's mask bit := ONE again
        BBZ, $IF,  $+1.0
        BBZ, PROT.0, OUTTGT
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        BZB, 6.0,  $                 ! hang if DS's routine did not run
        BBZ, 6.0,  $+1.0
        BB,  8.0,  $                 ! hang if IF's routine ran
        BB,  $IF,  $                 ! hang if IF's indicator is on
!
! ---- and the bit change was suppressed in all three --------------
!   Read it back with the system DISABLED, so this check cannot itself
!   signal.  Any block whose BBZ had completed would have cleared it.
        BD,  $+.32
        BZB, PROT.0, $               ! hang if the bit was cleared
        B,   PASS
PASS    BEW, $
!
! ---- the monitored area ------------------------------------------
!   The area is the HALF-OPEN interval [PROT, ABOVE) -- p.31: "all
!   words in subsequent locations up to, but not including, the
!   address in the upper boundary register."
        SLC, (8)4300.0
PROT    DD(BU,64,8), 0               ! == the lower boundary
        SLC, (8)4301.0
TGTIN   BEW, $                       ! blocks A and B's target: must NOT
                                     !   arrive
        SLC, (8)4302.0
ABOVE   DD(BU,64,8), 0               ! == the upper boundary, first word out
        SLC, (8)4360.0
OUTTGT  BEW, $                       ! block C's target: outside the area,
                                     !   and must still NOT arrive
!
! ---- the interruption table --------------------------------------
!   Entry N is at TBL + N words (p.48).  DS is 19, DF is 20, IF is 21.
!   All three exist even though only one fires per block: a missing
!   entry runs off into unloaded storage instead of failing at a check.
!   p.32: "When an interruption occurs, the resulting interruption
!   address is not monitored for instruction fetch", which is why the
!   table needs no special placement.
        SLC, (8)4500.0
TBL     DD, 0
        SLC, (8)4523.0
DSRTN   BB1, 6.0, $+1.0              ! entry 19 = DS  (0o4500 + 0o23)
        SLC, (8)4524.0
DFRTN   BB1, 7.0, $+1.0              ! entry 20 = DF  (0o4500 + 0o24)
        SLC, (8)4525.0
IFRTN   BB1, 8.0, $+1.0              ! entry 21 = IF  (0o4500 + 0o25)
        END, START
